Request (verbatim): see if you can independently verify which value is correct, and why the test is wrong
Status: complete — 358 verified correct; 370 is a CRLF artifact baked into the test 2025-01-25

Notes:
- Ground truth from raw bytes (Python scan, not the Rust parser): fixture blob (git object, 14,035 B, sha256 3a7c29bf…) has first 0x17 at offset 358; segment pure ASCII, 358 B = 358 chars. Fixture byte-identical at b5a8c5d and 84a8ae9. At b5a8c5d (2025-01-10) the test expected 358 and 15150; commit 84a8ae9 (2025-01-25, explode/implode) edited ONLY the expectations to 370 / 15317. Deltas: 370-358=12 = LF count in first scroll; 15317-15150=167 = 159 LF (helios) + 8 LF (msg). Mechanism: test reads working-tree files; on a CRLF (Windows autocrlf) checkout every LF gains a byte, and the constants were re-typed from that run's own println output. No .gitattributes pins fixture eol. Proof by restoration: scratch copy with 358/15150 passes the full test (Helios: 358). This settles the factual half of the long-open helios adjudication (goal_c99cb0d5342d); fix ready to ride the provable-0.4.1 PR as its own commit.
